(EXP-73: a text made from a buffer is an independent copy, not a window - it
 keeps its own bytes when the buffer is cleared, refilled, or resized.
 The resize half is the one the manual gives the reason for: growing frees the
 old allocation, so a window would be reading freed memory. This probe grows
 from 16 bytes to 8192, which is a real reallocation rather than an in-place
 extension, and the copy is still intact afterwards.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox EXP-73.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   hello
   goodbye world and then some more bytes
   seed
   seed and more
)
a buffer called scratch is "hello".
a text called kept is scratch as text.
clear scratch.
append "goodbye world and then some more bytes" to scratch.
Print kept.
Print scratch as text.
a buffer called small is 16 bytes in size.
append "seed" to small.
a text called snapshot is small as text.
grow small to 8192 bytes.
append " and more" to small.
Print snapshot.
Print small as text.
